vmwgfx: take read-only CPU locks with allow_cs so an unpaired read lock cannot wedge command submission

On kernel 6.12 every DRM_VMW_SYNCCPU grab without allow_cs raises the bo's cpu_writers,
and while it is non-zero every execbuf referencing the bo fails with -EBUSY, which Mesa
svga retries forever. Grabs are not tracked per drm_file, so AOSP screencap (locks its
buffer, exits) wedged SurfaceFlinger on device. Read locks now pass allow_cs: the grab still
waits for idle, the release is a no-op. Write locks are unchanged.

--- a/vmwgfx.c
+++ b/vmwgfx.c
@@ -486,6 +486,24 @@
 		arg.flags |= drm_vmw_synccpu_write;
 	if (!arg.flags)
 		arg.flags = drm_vmw_synccpu_read;
+	/*
+	 * A read-only lock only needs the GPU to be done with the buffer; it does
+	 * not need to stop later command submissions from touching it. Say so with
+	 * allow_cs. Without it the kernel counts the grab in vbo->cpu_writers, and
+	 * while that is non-zero every execbuf that references the buffer fails
+	 * validation with -EBUSY (vmw_validation_bo_validate_single), which Mesa
+	 * svga retries forever. The 6.12 driver does not track grabs per drm_file,
+	 * so a reader that never unlocks -- AOSP screencap locks its buffer and
+	 * exits -- leaves the count raised for the buffer's whole lifetime.
+	 * SurfaceFlinger still holds the capture buffer, so its next composition
+	 * wedged in the -EBUSY loop, then the system_server watchdog fired and
+	 * apps ANRed (measured on device). With allow_cs the grab still waits for
+	 * the buffer to go idle (dma_resv_wait_timeout) and the release is a
+	 * no-op, so an unpaired read lock cannot poison the buffer. Write locks
+	 * keep the counting grab: the UPDATE in bo_flush must not race the GPU.
+	 */
+	if (!(arg.flags & drm_vmw_synccpu_write))
+		arg.flags |= drm_vmw_synccpu_allow_cs;
 
 	ret = drmCommandWrite(bo->drv->fd, DRM_VMW_SYNCCPU, &arg, sizeof(arg));
 	if (ret)
